  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Where is the engine of a cable car?",
      "a": "The motor is in one of the stations, not in the cabins. It turns the bull wheel, which drives the rope loop.",
      "asks": [
        "Do cable cars have engines?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a bull wheel?",
      "a": "The bull wheel is the large wheel in a station that the haul rope wraps around. The motor turns it, and friction between wheel and rope pulls the rope along.",
      "asks": [
        "What turns the cable car rope?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How strong is a cable car rope?",
      "a": "Ropes are made of many twisted steel wires and are designed to be several times stronger than the biggest load they ever carry. They are inspected regularly.",
      "asks": [
        "What if the rope breaks?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do cabins attach to the rope?",
      "a": "Each cabin hangs from a grip that clamps onto the rope. On detachable systems the grip opens in the stations so cabins can slow down for people to get on and off.",
      "asks": [
        "How do gondolas slow down in the station?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a cable car need a tension weight?",
      "a": "Steel rope stretches with load and temperature. A heavy weight or hydraulic system pulls on the return wheel to keep the rope tight so it does not sag or slip.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a cable car rope kept tight?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Detachable gondola cabins let go of the moving rope in stations so passengers can board slowly.",
    "Cable car ropes are made of many steel wires twisted into strands.",
